A||||11||Robert||An acceptable risk…….||||5||
Z||000000||Robert||10-04-2001||07:59 PM||||My wife and I are headed from our home near San Francisco to the East coast this weekend.  We are visiting some old friends in Rhode Island/Vermont to check out the leaves and to play a little tennis.<BR>I’m OK with flying now, I think we take a bigger risk just driving to work each day.   It does all come down to the “acceptable risk” factor that we all live with everyday.  Just think what the world would be like if you were too afraid to leave your house each day?<BR>Besides, after 3 tours in Viet Nam, I think anyone messing with my trip will have a problem.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>Anyone else out there feel this way?<P>Later<BR>Robert<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||WampusCat||10-04-2001||08:21 PM||||I sure hope so!  When we first started flying after the WTC attacks, we were doing trips with anywhere from 6 to 35 passengers on a 148 passenger capacity aircraft.  My last trips to the west coast and back a few days ago had about 100 passengers each.  Not full but a definite improvement.<BR>  Before 9/11/01 the U.S. airlines adhered to a "common stategy" when dealing with hijackers.  It was assumend that the hijacker wanted to live and wanted to use the passengers and crew as a bargaining chip for some demand.  The "strategy" was to comply with the hijackers, take them wherever they wanted to go, fuel/weather permitting, land where ever they want to and let the professionals negotiate with them.<BR>  All that's changed now.  I think that anyone trying to hijack a plane now will have a life and death struggle on their hands from the passengers and the crew.<BR>  There's no official new "common strategy" yet but the general consensus seems to be to land at the nearest airport, disable the plane once safely on the ground and escape if possible.  Anybody bursting in the cockpit door these days is likely to get a crash ax between the eyes.<P>Steve, Northwest A320 First Officer||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever3000||10-04-2001||08:25 PM||||Flying is almost no risk at all.  Look at the number of airline flights every day before September 11.  Those hijacked planes created a spectacle the likes of which have never been seen, but still it was four flights out of the thousands in the air on any given day.  Look at the typical state lottery - it's the spectacular ones that get all the attention, when those matching five numbers for about $100,000 (for example) have much better odds.  <P>Disclaimer:  In no way am I making a comparison of the 9/11 tragedy to a stupid lottery.  My point was how peoples' attention gets focused on the shocking, not necessarily on the negligible chance of it happening to you.<p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||10-04-2001||08:42 PM||||I'll be dropping my younger brother off at an airport this weekend for a cross-country trip to visit our parents for a week. I haven't really thought about the risk of flying due to the high-jackings, but if I see any turbans in the terminal(I think thats what those things are called), I know my eyes will immedietly be centered on that person(s) until they are out of sight watching for some "weird" behavior. Call it unjust, or discriminating or whatever. But I know thats what I'd do. Wouldn't you?||||reg||1
Z||000004||fogglethorpe||10-04-2001||08:48 PM||||Flying is probably the safest mode of transportation at the moment.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||10-05-2001||09:33 AM||||I can't think of many places safer than a plane full of pissed-off patriotic Americans!  Can you imagine being on that plane today and 4 dude try to take over the plane with box cutters?  Oh, I don't think so!  ||||reg||6
Z||000006||Raydar||10-05-2001||09:45 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B>...but still it was four flights out of the thousands in the air </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Chuck,<BR>Only four flights because the FAA immediately shut down the air travel system. I think there were at least two more in the works. One was here in Atlanta, if you believe all the stories.<P>This was not meant to detract from your point at all. I agree completely.<P>And Steve, <BR>Yup, The crash axe between the eyes sounds reasonable to me. If they make it that far.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||10-05-2001||10:07 AM||||I wouldn't say it would be impossible for a hijacker to take a plane now, but I also do not see any organized terrorists trying that again anytime soon.  It would be too high-risk for their operation.<P>Everybody is in panic mode to keep airlines secure!  There are a lot more people watching over the airlines... and not just security.  Passengers, employees etc etc.  Any terrorist out there will know this, and try to think of another item he might be able to use instead.  So I would think they are rather safe now!<P>Have a safe trip!||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||10-05-2001||10:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>I haven't really thought about the risk of flying due to the high-jackings, but if I see any turbans in the terminal(I think thats what those things are called), I know my eyes will immedietly be centered on that person(s) until they are out of sight watching for some "weird" behavior. Call it unjust, or discriminating or whatever. But I know thats what I'd do. Wouldn't you?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Turbans are mst commonly worn by men from the indian state of Punjab of the Sikh religion. The Sikhs I have had the pleasure to know are without exception gentle, well educated people who have come to the United States to reap the rewards of a vibrant economy. They actually really like America and Americans (as do most Arabic people in the US). And Sikh do not worship Allah.<P>Just don't want all that hate to be misguided energy.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||10-05-2001||10:36 AM||||Stimpy, I think you're confusing hatred with common sense.  fiero56 didn't say he was going to shout hateful things at anyone he sees in a turban or assault that person...he said he would keep a watchful eye on them.<P>That just sounds like good sense to me.||||reg||5
Z||000010||Jaygee79||10-05-2001||12:09 PM||||I would've flown the next day if the planes were.  I refuse to live my life in fear.  Anything could happen on my way to work.  It doesn't have to take a terrorist on a plane.  Anyway, I would feel safer now that at any other time with all the added security.  Especially at Logan.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||10-06-2001||01:04 AM||||You got it right, Chad. I will be leaving for Columbus in just a few hours. Weekends are for living, weekdays are for sleeping(why am I "living" my weekend on a Fiero board). As long as I don't fall asleep on that boring a$$ highway we call I-70, it will be an uneventfull night.<P>Jaygee, I agree.||||reg||1
